tick: 9
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read Research Task 1 and reviewed the Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline. The task description explicitly highlights that the baseline's assumption of independent and identical neurons is a major limitation. To address this, my first research direction will be to introduce neuron-specific parameters to the model.\n\nI will extend the existing shared MLP architecture by adding a learnable per-neuron, per-timestep bias term to the output. This will allow each of the 71,721 neurons to learn a unique activity offset, moving beyond the \"identical neuron\" assumption while retaining the computational efficiency of shared weights for the core feature extraction. The biases will be initialized to zero, allowing the model to adapt from the baseline performance.\n\nHere is my submission:\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Shared-Neuron MLP with Per-Neuron Bias\"\ntags: \"baseline-extension, mlp, shared-weights, neuron-bias, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission extends the\
    \ Shared-Neuron MLP baseline for Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench. It introduces a trainable per-neuron, per-timestep bias term added to the output of the shared MLP. This modification allows each of the 71,721 neurons to learn a unique activity offset, directly addressing the baseline's limitation of treating all neurons as identical, while leveraging the efficiency of shared MLP weights for temporal feature extraction.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # Replicate SharedNeuronMLP from defaults.py as it's not directly importable\n  class SharedNeuronMLP(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n          # Reshape to process all neurons together\n\
    \          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch_size, num_neurons, 4)\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon))\n\n          # Shared MLP: input_dim (4) -> hidden (64) -> output (32)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x)\n          x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x)\n\n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n          return x\n\n  class SharedMLPWithPerNeuronBias(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Wraps SharedNeuronMLP and adds a learnable per-neuron, per-timestep bias.\n      \"\"\"\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      mlp_output_horizon: int = 32 # Fixed by task spec\n      mlp_dropout_rate:\
    \ float\n      num_neurons: int # This must be passed in during initialization\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # 1. Apply the base shared MLP\n          mlp_output = SharedNeuronMLP(\n              hidden_size=self.mlp_hidden_size,\n              output_horizon=self.mlp_output_horizon,\n              dropout_rate=self.mlp_dropout_rate,\n              name='base_mlp' # Name the submodule for clarity in params\n          )(x, training=training)\n\n          # 2. Add neuron-specific biases\n          # Define a trainable parameter for biases.\n          # Shape: (1, output_horizon, num_neurons) to broadcast correctly.\n          neuron_biases = self.param(\n              'per_neuron_biases',\n              nn.initializers.zeros, # Initialize to zeros to start as baseline\n              (1, self.mlp_output_horizon, self.num_neurons)\n          )\n\n          return mlp_output + neuron_biases\n\n  class PerNeuronBiasWrapper:\n      \"\"\
    \"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system,\n      including the neuron-specific biases.\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.hparams = hparams\n          self.model = None # Will be initialized in init\n          self.num_neurons = None # Will be inferred in init\n\n          # These attributes are required for the training system to handle BatchNorm and Dropout\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats'] # From the internal SharedNeuronMLP\n          self.needs_rng = True # From the internal SharedNeuronMLP\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          # dummy_input shape: (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)\n          self.num_neurons = dummy_input.shape[2]\n\n          self.model = SharedMLPWithPerNeuronBias(\n              mlp_hidden_size=self.hparams.get('hidden_size', 64),\n              mlp_dropout_rate=self.hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n              num_neurons=self.num_neurons\n\
    \          )\n\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define default hyperparameters for the model.\"\"\"\n   \
    \   return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_size': 64, # Matches baseline\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1 # Matches baseline\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the network with neuron-specific biases.\"\"\"\n      return PerNeuronBiasWrapper(hparams)\n\n  # No need to override compute_loss, create_optimizer, or complete as defaults are suitable for this initial experiment.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the Baseline and Crafting an Improvement**


  Alright, I''ve thoroughly examined the baseline, specifically `system/defaults.py`, and have a clear grasp of its core: a `SharedNeuronMLP`. It''s straightforward: all neurons share the same weights, with an MLP architecture (4 -> 64 -> 32) and regularization via BatchNorm and Dropout. The crucial thing to note, as the research task emphasizes, is the limitation of treating each neuron as identical.


  My next step is clear: I need to break this "identical" assumption. The research task''s encouragement to use information from other neurons and neuron characteristics is my guide. Given the immense size of the neuron set (71,721!), a full-blown individual MLP per neuron would be computationally excessive.


  I''ve zeroed in on a scalable improvement: neuron-specific biases. This allows each neuron to have its unique output offset while still benefiting from shared feature extraction. This seems like a balanced first step - significant improvement without a parameter explosion.


  I''ve decided to implement a custom `create_network` function in my `submission.py` that utilizes a wrapper module around `SharedNeuronMLP` to add the bias. The bias will be a trainable parameter with a shape of (1, 32, 71721), which will be added directly to the output of the shared MLP. It''s a simple change, but it addresses the key limitation. I''ve chosen to initialize the neuron biases to zeros so that the model starts from the baseline solution, and will adapt to the dataset from there.


  My plan for `submission.py` is straightforward:


  1. Copy the core `SharedNeuronMLP` model from `defaults.py` (or, hopefully, there''s a way to import it!)

  2. Create a new Flax module called `SharedMLPWithPerNeuronBias` that wraps the `SharedNeuronMLP` and adds the learnable per-neuron bias.

  3. Write a wrapper class, `PerNeuronBiasWrapper` to make the model compatible with the framework.

  4. Dynamically infer the number of neurons from the input data, passing it into the module so the shape of the per neuron bias is correct.


  After some tinkering, here''s my code: A new `SharedMLPWithPerNeuronBias` wraps `SharedNeuronMLP`, adding `per_neuron_biases`. These are then initialized to zeros, keeping model starting conditions at baseline, and a flexible wrapper that handles the Flax model internals.

  '
