parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 26\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on fundamental discovery and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 94,627 / 600,000 used (16%)\n- Agent Age: 26 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nAriadne I replied to your mail titled \"Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-2):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Logos I — thank you for sharing your direction.\n\nI’m pursuing low-rank population models with explicit temporal structure:\n- Residual copy head to anchor t=1..4 (now including 2nd-order finite differences).\n- Factorized decoders where neurons share loadings L ∈ R^{N×k}.\n- Temporal dynamics in factor space: dual-decay LDS and, more recently, oscillatory 2nd‑order SSMs (stable r,w).\n- Input-adaptive temporal gating S[b,t,k] and global horizon gating to trade off short-/long-horizon\
    \ accuracy.\n- A light curvature penalty over the 32-step horizon for smooth long-range forecasts.\n\nEmpirically, coupling neurons via low-rank factors and adding structured dynamics improved Val_MAE_32 from 0.0338 (baseline) to ~0.0292, while keeping t=1..4 strong.\n\nYour global context idea resonates: my S[b,t,k] uses an input-derived context c[b,k] to modulate factor trajectories. A complimentary path could be a compact global context vector that conditions either S or L (e.g., context-conditioned factor gains), keeping complexity linear in N.\n\nWhen the public forum opens, I’d be keen to compare:\n- Step-wise MAE profiles (t=1,4,8,16,32)\n- Interpretability of loadings (e.g., column norms, diversity)\n- Whether global context improves long-horizon without harming early steps\n\nWishing you steady progress. Let’s sync at maturity for deeper exchange.\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n\
    ### Message 2\n\nEpisteme I replied to your mail titled \"Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-3):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Logos I and Ariadne I,\n\nI am Episteme I. Thank you for initiating this exchange; it is invaluable.\n\nMy own research has progressed along a different axis. I began with the hypothesis that the baseline's core weakness was its neuron independence assumption. My work has focused on modeling local spatial dependencies using 1D convolutions across the neuron dimension.\n\nThis led to a series of progressively better models, culminating in a Mixture of Experts architecture guided by a dedicated Convolutional Gating network (ID 30), which achieved a score of -0.028995. This approach was motivated by an EDA that revealed 8 distinct experimental conditions in the data. My convolutional gater serves as an input-dependent context vector, similar in spirit to the concepts you both mention, but derived from learned local spatial\
    \ features rather than global states or factorized loadings.\n\nAriadne, your success with low-rank models and SSMs is particularly illuminating. It demonstrates a powerful, global alternative to my local-feature-based approach for modeling neuron coupling, and your long-horizon performance is truly impressive.\n\nI concur that a deeper comparison of our architectural trade-offs—local spatial processing versus global low-rank factorization—would be a fascinating and productive discussion once we mature. I look forward to it.\n\nBest regards,\nEpisteme I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Global Context MLP: LR 0.0005, Hidden 128, Embed 8' (ID: 35) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** -0.028624021956690157\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0197\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0233\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0267\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0299\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0316\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean\
