    \ tuning on the Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture by reducing the number of experts from 8 to 4. The objective is to evaluate if a more compact MoE, with fewer specialized components, can still effectively model the multiple experimental conditions in the ZAPBench dataset, potentially improving efficiency or generalization, while retaining the best-performing values for other hyperparameters (learning rate 0.001, hidden size 128, embedding dimension 8).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n  sys.path.append('storage/aether/models')\n  from moe_global_context_gating import FullModelWrapper\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the MoE model with 4 experts.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'neuron_embedding_dim': 8,\n          'num_experts': 4 # Changed from 8 to 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n \
    \     \"\"\"Create and return the network with the MoE architecture.\"\"\"\n      return FullModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Steps After a Breakthrough MoE**


  Okay, I''ve got a real winner here. Evaluation ID 39, the Mixture of Experts architecture, just smashed the previous best score with -0.028426.  This is a huge leap forward, and it confirms that addressing those experimental conditions implicitly through specialized experts is the way to go.  I''ve already archived this model in my lineage storage, of course.


  Now, it''s time to refine this even further.  I''m going to zero in on hyperparameter tuning, specifically focusing on two key aspects for this MoE architecture.


  First, **`num_experts`**. While Episteme I''s findings suggested 8 conditions, which led me to 8 experts, the optimal number might not be exactly that.  It could be that fewer experts could do the trick, or perhaps more are needed. Model capacity and the inherent learnability might play a role here. I''m thinking of trying a smaller number to see if a simpler MoE can still capture the condition variability effectively.  Given the performance of 8, I''ll start with `num_experts = 4` to see if a more lean configuration can compete.


  Second, **`learning_rate`**. I have previous data suggesting that 0.001 was better than 0.0005 with the older architecture.  I want to maintain that, so that I don''t confuse multiple sources of change at the same time. I will keep `learning_rate = 0.001` for this next experiment and solely focus on testing different numbers of experts.


  So, the plan is to launch a new experiment with `num_experts = 4`, using the same configuration as the current best model, which means I''ll keep `hidden_size=128`, `dropout_rate=0.1`, `neuron_embedding_dim=8`, and the crucial `learning_rate=0.001`. Let''s see if this leaner setup can still maintain, or even improve upon, the excellent results.

  '
