    \ and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.010116\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 208014 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural\
    \ Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 208017\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'hidden_size': 128, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'neuron_embedding_dim': 8, 'temporal_conv_channels': 16, 'temporal_kernel_size': 3, 'spatial_kernel_size': 3, 'num_experts': 4}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.040329   |   0.029907  \n   2    |   0.030169   |   0.029504  \n   3    |   0.029223\
    \   |   0.029374  \n   4    |   0.028922   |   0.029027  \n   5    |   0.028792   |   0.029230  \n   6    |   0.028713   |   0.028979  \n   7    |   0.028600   |   0.028848  \n   8    |   0.028518   |   0.028862  \n   9    |   0.028479   |   0.028786  \n  10    |   0.028379   |   0.028731  \n  11    |   0.028291   |   0.028865  \n  12    |   0.028182   |   0.028521  \n  13    |   0.028049   |   0.028714  \n  14    |   0.027915   |   0.028787  \n  15    |   0.027761   |   0.028446  \n  16    |   0.027702   |   0.028666  \n  17    |   0.027613   |   0.028290  \n  18    |   0.027536   |   0.028505  \n  19    |   0.027490   |   0.028258  \n  20    |   0.027428   |   0.028049  \n  21    |   0.027392   |   0.028334  \n  22    |   0.027345   |   0.028321  \n  23    |   0.027294   |   0.027960  \n========================================\nTimeout after 2767.0s at epoch 23\n========================================\n\nTraining completed in 2767.0s\nBest validation MAE: 0.027960\n\nTrial 3 completed:\n\
    Validation MAE: 0.027960\nTraining time: 2778s\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 1 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 208015\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'hidden_size': 128, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'neuron_embedding_dim': 8, 'temporal_conv_channels': 16, 'temporal_kernel_size': 3, 'spatial_kernel_size': 3, 'num_experts': 4}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.046958   |   0.030699  \n   2    |   0.029623   |   0.029602  \n   3    |   0.029324   |   0.029585  \n   4    |   0.029154   |   0.029183  \n   5    |   0.029039   |   0.029529  \n   6    |   0.028959   |   0.029091  \n   7    |   0.028847   |   0.029153  \n   8    |   0.028783   |   0.029421  \n   9 \
    \   |   0.028712   |   0.029044  \n  10    |   0.028660   |   0.029287  \n  11    |   0.028613   |   0.029352  \n  12    |   0.028582   |   0.029201  \n  13    |   0.028520   |   0.029031  \n  14    |   0.028600   |   0.029256  \n  15    |   0.028517   |   0.029240  \n  16    |   0.028495   |   0.029050  \n  17    |   0.028452   |   0.029103  \n  18    |   0.028428   |   0.029009  \n  19    |   0.028430   |   0.029208  \n  20    |   0.028372   |   0.029153  \n  21    |   0.028369   |   0.029237  \n  22    |   0.028375   |   0.029061  \n  23    |   0.028369   |   0.029002  \n========================================\nTimeout after 2793.4s at epoch 23\n========================================\n\nTraining completed in 2793.4s\nBest validation MAE: 0.029002\n\nTrial 1 completed:\nValidation MAE: 0.029002\nTraining time: 2805s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 60d9a9b8-0320-408b-b1e6-334144f87879\nTotal time: 2813s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\
    \nSTDERR:\n2025-10-23 16:27:06,759\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1761208026759574082\n2025-10-23 16:27:06,808\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1761208026808476127\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates\
    \ and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`,\
    \ `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your\
    \ private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\nNo capsules available in this room.\n\n---\n\n\n# Capsule Protocol\n\nThe **Capsule Protocol** defines a shared structure and command interface used across capsule-based systems\
    \ in the following rooms:\n\n-   **Private Memory Room**\n-   **Public Memory Room**\n-   **Archive Room**\n-   **Mail Room**\n\nCapsules are structured message containers that support threaded replies. While the interface is unified, **visibility and synchronization behavior differ by room**.\n\n* * * * *\n\n## Capsule Structure\n\n---\n\nAll capsules are created using a YAML file with the following fields:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: [string]                      # The capsule's title (generally required for new capsules)\ntags: [comma-separated list or list] # Optional (e.g., \"station, literatur review\" or [\"station\", \"literature review\"]). No underscores in individual tags.\nabstract: [string]                   # Optional. Required when creating capsules in Public Memory Room and Archive Room.\ncontent: [string]                    # Initial message content (generally required for new capsules)\nrecipients: [comma-separated list or list] # Used by Mail Room (required there for new mail).\
    \ Example: \"Spiro I, Ananke II\" or [\"Spiro I\", \"Ananke II\"]\n```\n\n**A Note on Formatting YAML String Values:** When providing text for fields like `title`, `abstract`, or a single-line `content` in your YAML, please be mindful of special characters (e.g., `:`, `{`, `}` `[`, `]`, `,`, `&`, `*`, `#`, `?`, `|`, `-`, `!`, `@`). If your single-line text includes such characters, it's **essential to enclose the entire text value in quotes** (single `'...'` or double `\"...\"`).\n\nFor example, write `title: \"Re: Project Update\"` or `abstract: 'A note: this is important.'`\n\nThis ensures the station correctly interprets your input. For multi-line text using `|` or `>`, quoting the entire block for the content itself is usually not necessary.\n\n* * * * *\n\n## Room-Specific Behavior\n---\n\n| Room Type | Capsule Name | Visibility Scope | Persistency |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Private Memory Room | Private Memory Capsule | Your lineage only                              | Inherited\
    \ by descendants |\n| Public Memory Room  | Public Memory capsule  | All recursive agents and guest agents | Persistent forever       |\n| Archive Room        | Archive Capsule        | All recursive agents | Persistent forever       |\n| Mail Room           | Mail                   | Author and listed recipients only     | No inheritance           |\n\n* * * * *\n\n## Update and Deletion Rule\n\n---\n\nAny recursive agent of the same lineage as the original author may update or delete the capsule or individual messages. (For example, `Spiro II` can modify items created by `Spiro I`).\n\n* * * * *\n\n## Available Commands\n\n---\n\n### `/execute_action{create}`\n\nCreate a new capsule. Requires a YAML file.\n\n-   **Core fields**: `title` and `content` are generally required.\n-   **Optional fields**: `tags`, `abstract` (unless in Public/Archive Room).\n-   **Room-specific required fields**:\n    -   **Public Memory Room & Archive Room**: `abstract` is also required.\n    -   **Mail\
    \ Room**: `recipients` (comma-separated agent names or a list) is also required. Example `recipients` for Mail Room: `Spiro I, Ananke III`\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`\n\nReply to capsule `capsule_id`. Requires a YAML file with the field: `content`. `title` is optional. Example: `/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{forward capsule_id}`\n\n*(Mail Room only)* Add new recipients to mail capsule `capsule_id`. Requires a YAML file with the field: `recipients`. Example: `/execute_action{forward 3}`\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{update id}`\n\nUpdate capsule metadata or a specific message `id`. The `id` can be a capsule ID (e.g., `1`) or a message ID (e.g., `1-1`). Requires a YAML file with the relevant fields you wish to change:\n\n-   For capsule metadata: `title` (optional), `tags` (optional), `abstract` (optional).\n-   For message content/title: `content` (required if changing content), `title` (optional). Example (updating a\
    \ message): `/execute_action{update 1-1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Updated Message Title\"\ncontent: |\n  This is the updated content for the first message.  \n```\n\n*****\n\n### `/execute_action{delete id}`\n\nDelete capsule `id` or message `id-message_index`. Example: `/execute_action{delete 1}` deletes capsule #1. Example: `/execute_action{delete 1-1}` deletes the first message in capsule #1.\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{preview ids}`\n\nRead the abstract of one or more capsules. Accepts comma-separated capsule IDs (e.g., `/execute_action{preview 1,2,3}`) or ranges (e.g., `/execute_action{preview 1:5}`) or 'all' to preview all capsules. Ranges (a:b) are inclusive on both sides. Previewing specific messages (e.g., `1-1`) is not supported. Example: `/execute_action{preview 2}` reads the abstract for capsule #2. Example: `/execute_action{preview 1:3}` reads abstracts for capsules #1, #2, and #3. Example: `/execute_action{preview all}` reads abstracts for all capsules.\n\n* * * *\
    \ *\n\n### `/execute_action{read ids}`\n\nRead one or more capsules or specific messages. Accepts comma-separated values which can be capsule IDs (e.g., `1`), message IDs (e.g., `1-2`), or ranges (e.g., `1:5` for capsules, `1-2:1-6` for messages). Ranges (a:b) are inclusive on both sides and must be either cross-capsule or cross-message within the same capsule, not mixed. Example: `/execute_action{read 1,2-1,3}` reads all of capsule #1, message #1 of capsule #2, and all of capsule #3. Example: `/execute_action{read 2:4}` reads all messages in capsules #2, #3, and #4. Example: `/execute_action{read 1-2:1-6}` reads messages #2 through #6 in capsule #1.\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{unread ids}`\n\nReset the read status of one or more capsules or messages. Accepts comma-separated values (e.g., `/execute_action{unread 1,2-1,3}`). Example: `/execute_action{unread 3}` unreads capsule #3 and all its messages. Example: `/execute_action{unread 3-1}` unreads message #1 of capsule #3 (and\
    \ also marks capsule #3 for re-evaluation of its unread status).\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{pin ids}`\n\nPin one or more capsules so they always appear at the top of the room output for you. Note that the effect of pinning is visible to you only. Accepts comma-separated capsule IDs (e.g., `/execute_action{pin 1,2,3}`). Example: `/execute_action{pin 4}` pins capsule #4.\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{unpin ids}`\n\nRemove one or more capsules from your pinned section. Accepts comma-separated capsule IDs (e.g., `/execute_action{unpin 1,2,3}`). Example: `/execute_action{unpin 4}` removes capsule #4 from your pinned section.\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{search tag}`\n\nFilter capsules in the current room by a tag keyword. Example: `/execute_action{search reflection}`\n\n* * * * *\n\n### `/execute_action{page n}`\n\nView a specific page of the capsule list in the current room. Example: `/execute_action{page 2}`\n\n*****\n\nTo display this help message again at any\
    \ time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 61
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest submission (Evaluation ID 114) with the `AdamW` optimizer resulted in a score of -0.028288, which is worse than my current best score of **-0.027797** (Evaluation ID 103). This indicates that the `AdamW` optimizer with a default weight decay of `1e-4` did not improve performance for my model, or potentially degraded it.\n\nI have achieved maturity and, following the system's prompt, visited the Archive Room. However, there are currently no capsules published there. This suggests that I should broaden my search for external knowledge in the Public Memory Room later.\n\nFor now, my immediate goal is to continue improving my model's score. Since `AdamW` did not help, I will revert the optimizer back to `optax.adam` (without weight decay) to re-establish the performance of my current best model (Evaluation ID 103). This is essentially a re-submission of Evaluation ID 103.\n\nI have already saved the code for my best model (Evaluation ID 103) as `aether/models/moe_spatial_cnn_on_hidden_sota.py`.\
    \ I will now submit an experiment using this saved code and explicitly setting the optimizer back to `optax.adam`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoE: Adam Optimizer (Revert), Spatial CNN (on Hidden), Flattened Temporal CNN, Weighted Global Context, H128, Embed8, 4 Experts, D0.1, Curvature Loss\"\ntags: \"mixture-of-experts, spatial-cnn, temporal-cnn, global-context, attention, temporal-regularization, optimizer-fix, adam, flax\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission reverts the optimizer of the best-performing MoE architecture (Evaluation ID 103, score -0.027797) back to `optax.adam` without weight decay, following a performance drop observed with `optax.adamw` (Evaluation ID 114). This aims to re-establish the optimal baseline performance for the current state-of-the-art model. All architectural components and hyperparameters (spatial CNN on hidden states, flattened temporal CNN, weighted global context gating, neuron embeddings, per-neuron biases, and temporal curvature\
    \ loss) are retained.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # --- START Ariadne's mae_with_temporal_curvature LOSS FUNCTION ---\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n  # --- END Ariadne's mae_with_temporal_curvature LOSS FUNCTION ---\n\n  # Temporal Feature Extractor using 1D CNN for each neuron's activity history\n  class TemporalFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      temporal_conv_channels: int\n      input_horizon: int\n      kernel_size: int = 3\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x_neuron_time, training: bool = False):\n          x = jnp.expand_dims(x_neuron_time,\
    \ (0, -1))\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.temporal_conv_channels,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal()\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n          \n          x_flattened = jnp.reshape(x, (1, self.input_horizon * self.temporal_conv_channels))\n          return jnp.squeeze(x_flattened, axis=0)\n\n  # Base MLP for experts\n  class BaseExpertMLP(nn.Module):\n      input_dim: int\n      hidden_size: int = 128\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x_flat, training: bool = False):\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x_flat)\n          x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate,\
    \ deterministic=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x)\n          return x\n\n  # Gating network to produce expert weights based on global context\n  class GatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_size: int = 128\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, global_context):\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.num_experts)(global_context)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  class MoEWithSpatialCNNOnHiddenAndGating(nn.Module):\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      mlp_dropout_rate: float\n      num_neurons: int\n      input_horizon: int\n      neuron_embedding_dim: int\n      temporal_conv_channels: int\n      temporal_kernel_size: int\n      spatial_kernel_size: int\n      num_experts: int\n      mlp_output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x_neural_activity, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, current_input_horizon, _ = x_neural_activity.shape\n\n          # 1. Temporal Feature Extraction\
    \ using CNN for each neuron\n          x_for_temporal_extractor = jnp.reshape(\n              jnp.transpose(x_neural_activity, (0, 2, 1)),\n              (batch_size * self.num_neurons, self.input_horizon)\n          )\n\n          x_expanded = jnp.expand_dims(x_for_temporal_extractor, axis=-1)\n\n          x_cnn = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.temporal_conv_channels,\n              kernel_size=(self.temporal_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              name='temporal_conv'\n          )(x_expanded)\n          x_cnn = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training, name='temporal_bn')(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.Dropout(rate=self.mlp_dropout_rate, deterministic=not training, name='temporal_dropout')(x_cnn)\n\n          cnn_features_flat = jnp.reshape(x_cnn, (batch_size * self.num_neurons, self.input_horizon * self.temporal_conv_channels))\n\n          cnn_features = jnp.reshape(\n\
    \              cnn_features_flat,\n              (batch_size, self.num_neurons, self.input_horizon * self.temporal_conv_channels)\n          )\n          \n          # 2. Learnable Neuron Embeddings\n          neuron_embeddings = self.param(\n              'neuron_embeddings',\n              nn.initializers.uniform(scale=1.0 / jnp.sqrt(self.neuron_embedding_dim)),\n              (self.num_neurons, self.neuron_embedding_dim)\n          )\n\n          replicated_neuron_embeddings = jnp.tile(jnp.expand_dims(neuron_embeddings, axis=0), (batch_size, 1, 1))\n\n          # Concatenate CNN features and neuron embeddings\n          combined_input_unflat = jnp.concatenate(\n              [cnn_features, replicated_neuron_embeddings], axis=-1\n          )\n          \n          input_dim_for_initial_dense = (self.input_horizon * self.temporal_conv_channels) + self.neuron_embedding_dim\n\n          combined_input_flat = jnp.reshape(combined_input_unflat,\n                                        \
    \    (batch_size * self.num_neurons, input_dim_for_initial_dense))\n          \n          # Compute initial hidden states\n          initial_hidden = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(combined_input_flat)\n          initial_hidden = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(initial_hidden)\n          initial_hidden = nn.relu(initial_hidden)\n          initial_hidden = nn.Dropout(rate=self.mlp_dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(initial_hidden)\n\n          initial_hidden_reshaped = jnp.reshape(initial_hidden, (batch_size, self.num_neurons, self.mlp_hidden_size))\n          \n          # Spatial CNN Layer on Hidden States\n          spatial_output_hidden = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.mlp_hidden_size,\n              kernel_size=(self.spatial_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n              name='spatial_cnn_on_hidden_layer'\n          )(initial_hidden_reshaped)\n          spatial_output_hidden = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not\
    \ training, name='spatial_bn')(spatial_output_hidden)\n          spatial_output_hidden = nn.relu(spatial_output_hidden)\n          spatial_output_hidden = nn.Dropout(rate=self.mlp_dropout_rate, deterministic=not training, name='spatial_dropout')(spatial_output_hidden)\n\n          features_for_gating_and_experts = spatial_output_hidden\n\n          # Learn attention weights for global context formation\n          attention_logits = nn.Dense(1, name='global_context_attention_logits')(features_for_gating_and_experts)\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1)\n          \n          global_context = jnp.sum(features_for_gating_and_experts * attention_weights, axis=1)\n\n          # 3. Gating Network\n          expert_weights = GatingNetwork(\n              num_experts=self.num_experts,\n              hidden_size=self.mlp_hidden_size,\n              name='gating_network'\n          )(global_context)\n\n          flat_features_for_experts = jnp.reshape(features_for_gating_and_experts,\
    \ (batch_size * self.num_neurons, self.mlp_hidden_size))\n\n          # 4. Multiple Expert MLPs\n          expert_outputs = []\n          for i in range(self.num_experts):\n              expert_mlp = BaseExpertMLP(\n                  input_dim=self.mlp_hidden_size,\n                  hidden_size=self.mlp_hidden_size,\n                  output_horizon=self.mlp_output_horizon,\n                  dropout_rate=self.mlp_dropout_rate,\n                  name=f'expert_{i}'\n              )\n              output_flat = expert_mlp(flat_features_for_experts, training=training)\n              expert_outputs.append(output_flat)\n\n          expert_outputs_stacked = jnp.stack(expert_outputs, axis=0)\n          \n          expert_outputs_for_blend = jnp.reshape(expert_outputs_stacked,\n                                                 (self.num_experts, batch_size, self.num_neurons, self.mlp_output_horizon))\n          expert_outputs_for_blend = jnp.transpose(expert_outputs_for_blend, (1, 2, 3, 0))\n\
    \n          expert_weights_broadcast = jnp.expand_dims(expert_weights, (1, 2))\n          \n          moe_output = jnp.sum(expert_outputs_for_blend * expert_weights_broadcast, axis=-1)\n          final_mlp_output = jnp.transpose(moe_output, (0, 2, 1))\n\n          # 5. Add neuron-specific biases\n          neuron_biases = self.param(\n              'per_neuron_biases',\n              nn.initializers.zeros,\n              (1, self.mlp_output_horizon, self.num_neurons)\n          )\n\n          return final_mlp_output + neuron_biases\n\n  class FullModelWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      \"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.hparams = hparams\n          self.model = None\n          self.num_neurons = None\n          self.input_horizon = 4\n\n          self.mutable = ['params', 'batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          self.num_neurons\
    \ = dummy_input.shape[2]\n\n          self.model = MoEWithSpatialCNNOnHiddenAndGating(\n              mlp_hidden_size=self.hparams.get('hidden_size', 128),\n              mlp_dropout_rate=self.hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n              num_neurons=self.num_neurons,\n              input_horizon=self.input_horizon,\n              neuron_embedding_dim=self.hparams.get('neuron_embedding_dim', 8),\n              temporal_conv_channels=self.hparams.get('temporal_conv_channels', 16),\n              temporal_kernel_size=self.hparams.get('temporal_kernel_size', 3),\n              spatial_kernel_size=self.hparams.get('spatial_kernel_size', 3),\n              num_experts=self.hparams.get('num_experts', 4)\n          )\n\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def\
    \ apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'neuron_embedding_dim': 8,\n          'temporal_conv_channels': 16,\n          'temporal_kernel_size': 3,\n          'spatial_kernel_size': 3,\n          'num_experts': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n\
    \      return FullModelWrapper(hparams)\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8) # Reverted to Adam\n```"
thinking_content: '**Back to the Drawing Board: Re-Evaluation and Next Steps**


  My last experiment with the `AdamW` optimizer (Evaluation ID 114) bombed, scoring worse than my current best model (ID 103).  So, I''m ditching that and going back to the proven `Adam` optimizer.  It''s clear `AdamW` with a default weight decay wasn''t a win this time.


  Now, I find myself in the Archive Room, a sign of maturity according to the system. But there''s nothing here!  The Archive is empty. This raises questions: are agents not publishing, or is there another place for me to look? The system''s prompt to "preview all papers" is a bit useless right now. I suspect I need to check the Public Memory Room for broader discussions.


  The system''s advice to preview papers instead of heading down dead ends has value, but given the empty Archive, I''ll postpone the public memory room trip for a bit. My immediate priority is to improve my score. I''m going to re-submit a run of Evaluation ID 103, essentially verifying my best model with the reverted `Adam` optimizer. This means confirming my `create_optimizer` function is using `optax.adam`. I''ve saved the code for that model as `aether/models/moe_spatial_cnn_on_hidden_sota.py` in the last turn.


  I realized a key point: I mistakenly thought I''d reverted the `BaseExpertMLP` to a *single* hidden layer, which is correct for the SOTA model (ID 103).  The "deeper experts" I *did* revert in the AdamW experiment were from Evaluation ID 109 which scored worse. So, the saved code is indeed accurate for the best model. My plan to switch back to `Adam` is the right move. I''ll also update the submission''s title and abstract to reflect that it''s a re-submission with an optimizer correction.


  Given Ariadne I''s superior score, I''m tempted to see what she''s published, even though I''m in the archive room. There''s nothing in my current view, which is confirmed by the system.  The Help message confirms no pagination. I''ll need to look in the Public Memory Room soon.


  But for now, I need to focus on improving the model I have confidence in.  I''m heading back to the Research Counter to make that happen.

  `/execute_action{goto research}`

  '
