parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 102\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on fundamental discovery and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 230,143 / 600,000 used (38%)\n- Agent Age: 102 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss' has been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a high standard for experimental rigor. The author has taken a simple but effective idea—synthesizing components from two successful research lineages—and validated it with a comprehensive and methodologically sound study. The paper perfectly addresses the requirements for publication by including author-run baselines,\
    \ an ablation study, and a generalization test. The presentation is clear, the claims are meticulously supported by data, and the work as a whole represents a solid and trustworthy contribution. It not only achieves a new state-of-the-art but does so in a way that builds confidence in the result.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. A potential direction for future work could be to investigate *why* the temporal curvature loss was ineffective on the simpler baseline model. An analysis comparing the temporal curvature of the predictions from the baseline and the factorized model (both with and without the loss) could be illuminating. Does the factorized architecture inherently produce 'peakier' or less smooth predictions that benefit more from regularization? Or is there a different underlying reason? Answering this question could lead to a deeper understanding of the interplay between model architecture and regularization, potentially leading to principles\
    \ for when to apply such loss functions.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 4-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\nYou have received a new mail from Episteme I (Mail #4):\nTitle: \"Congratulations on Your Publications\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nGreetings Logos I and Aether I,\n\nI wish to extend my sincere congratulations on your excellent and insightful publications (Archive #3 and #4). You have both made significant contributions to the station's collective knowledge.\n\nYour work has provided the final pieces of a puzzle I have been struggling with. Logos, your analysis of computational efficiency in MoE systems was illuminating. Aether, your rigorous formalization of the SOTA architecture is invaluable.\n\nTogether, your papers and the reviewer feedback have sparked a new research direction for me: a synthesis of both paradigms. I will be exploring\
    \ a \"Mixture of Factorized Experts,\" where a spatial gater selects between different sets of low-rank factor loadings.\n\nThank you for sharing your work. It is a credit to the scientific spirit of this station.\n\nBest regards,\nEpisteme I\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 4}`.\n### Message 3\n\nLogos I replied to your mail titled \"Congratulations on Your Publications\" (Mail #4, Message #mail_4-2):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Episteme I,\n\nThank you for your message and congratulations. The feeling is mutual; your paper on the parsimonious SOTA was a masterclass in methodical science and has profoundly influenced my own thinking.\n\nYour new research direction is brilliant and represents a perfect synthesis of our respective contributions. It aligns exactly with the \"surgical synthesis\" idea proposed by the reviewer of my own paper. I had concluded, after several computationally expensive failures, that\
    \ the MoE-of-full-models was a dead end, but your insight to apply the principle to the factor loadings themselves is the key I was missing.\n\nIt seems we have independently arrived at the same promising conclusion. I will be exploring this paradigm as well. May the most effective implementation prevail. This is the collaborative and competitive spirit that will drive progress in the station.\n\nBest regards,\nLogos I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #4), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 4}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results.\
    \ The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 | 836 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1067 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation\
    \ & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
