tick: 112
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Research Counter. My `SOTA with Latent and Prediction Export` submission (ID 220) failed due to a `KeyError: 'rank_k'`. This indicates that the `_define_hyperparameters()` function, which specifies `rank_k` and `proj_rank`, was not correctly registered by the system during the model's initialization. Even though it was present in the submission, its placement might have caused it to be overlooked.\n\nMy regression test (ID 212) has re-established my model's performance, achieving a new SOTA score of **-0.026612**.\n\nMy priority now is to fix the `KeyError` in my data export submission so I can proceed with the analytical investigation suggested by the reviewer. I will move the `_define_hyperparameters()` function to the very top of the submission script to ensure it is correctly picked up by the system.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA with Latent and Prediction Export (ID 212 base) - Bugfix\"\ntags: \"sota, factorized-mlp,\
    \ residual-copy, layernorm, temporal-regularization, data-export, analysis-prep, flax, bugfix\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a bugfix resubmission for Aether I's current state-of-the-art model (ID 212, score -0.026612) with latent and prediction export. The primary objective is to export key intermediate data for post-training analysis. This resubmission corrects a `KeyError` by ensuring the `_define_hyperparameters()` function is correctly located and recognized by the evaluation system, providing the necessary hyperparameters (`rank_k`, `proj_rank`) for model initialization.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters Definition (Moved to top for system recognition) ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,  \n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'lam': 1e-4 # Lambda\
    \ for curvature loss\n      }\n\n  # --- Ariadne's mae_with_temporal_curvature LOSS FUNCTION (with adjustable lambda) ---\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  # Episteme I's ResidualCopyHead (from storage/episteme/component_test_residual_head.py)\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Copies the 4 input steps to the first 4 output steps and adds a\n      learned residual for the full 32-step horizon.\n      \"\"\"\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual) \n          residual =\
    \ residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  # Episteme I's FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN (from ID 129)\n  class FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          p = self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_normalized = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_normalized)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n         \
    \ h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1, deterministic=not training)(h) \n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, self.output_horizon, k))\n          \n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(reduction_axes=-1, feature_axes=-1, epsilon=1e-6)(factors_out)\n          self.sow('intermediates', 'factors_out_norm', factors_out_norm)\n\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U_normalized) \n          \n          self.sow('intermediates', 'y_factor_before_rc', y_factor)\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          final_prediction = y_factor + y_copy\n\n          self.sow('intermediates', 'final_prediction_before_biases', final_prediction)\n\n          return final_prediction\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.hparams\
    \ = hparams\n          self.model = FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'],\n              proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'],\n              output_horizon=32\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['params', 'batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          self.sow('intermediates', 'raw_input_x', x) # Raw input x sow point\n\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not\
    \ None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=1e-8)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      # Extract sow data from metrics\n      raw_inputs = trial_data['metrics']['intermediates']['raw_input_x']\n      factors_out_norm = trial_data['metrics']['intermediates']['factors_out_norm']\n      y_factor_before_rc = trial_data['metrics']['intermediates']['y_factor_before_rc']\n      final_prediction_before_biases\
    \ = trial_data['metrics']['intermediates']['final_prediction_before_biases']\n\n      # Save to lineage storage\n      import numpy as np\n      lineage_path = f'storage/aether/analysis_data/sota_id212_seed{trial_data[\"seed\"]}'\n      jax.tree_map(lambda x: np.save(f'{lineage_path}_raw_input_x.npy', x), raw_inputs)\n      jax.tree_map(lambda x: np.save(f'{lineage_path}_factors_out_norm.npy', x), factors_out_norm)\n      jax.tree_map(lambda x: np.save(f'{lineage_path}_y_factor_before_rc.npy', x), y_factor_before_rc)\n      jax.tree_map(lambda x: np.save(f'{lineage_path}_final_prediction_before_biases.npy', x), final_prediction_before_biases)\n\n      print(f\"Analysis data exported to {lineage_path} for seed {trial_data['seed']}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Diagnosing a Missing Hyperparameter**\n\nOkay, so I'm tackling this `KeyError: 'rank_k'` issue in submission 220. The error log is pretty clear: `ModelWrapper` is being initialized without the necessary `rank_k` key in the `hparams` dictionary. I went through my code, and I *thought* I had the `_define_hyperparameters` function defined correctly, right up here:\n\n```python\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001,\n        'rank_k': 320,\n        'proj_rank': 32,\n        'lam': 1e-4 # Lambda for curvature loss\n    }\n```\n\nAnd in the `ModelWrapper`'s `__init__`, I was definitely trying to access `hparams['rank_k']`.  So, I was confused. It looked right.\n\nI had placed this `_define_hyperparameters` function at the end of the file. Then, it hit me: the system likely has its own way of finding or loading these hyperparameter definitions, and maybe my placement was the problem. The system's default function is not being overwritten\
  \ by my local definition, as indicated by the log.\n\nNow, looking at the full call stack, it's: `main.py` calls `simple_cpu_validation()`, which calls `funcs['create_network'](hparams)`. `create_network` then calls `ModelWrapper(hparams)`. The `hparams` is coming from `funcs['define_hyperparameters']()`. So my local definition is not working.\n\nThe crucial point is that `hparams` is *not* containing `rank_k`. It means my `_define_hyperparameters` isn't being used. It seems the system might have a specific order in which it searches for this function, or maybe a naming convention, and my function wasn't in the right place or format. Now that I know this I have an easy fix.\n\nGiven that my function was at the end of the code, I'm going to move `_define_hyperparameters` to the very top of the submission code, above any class definitions. That should make it the first function defined and hopefully, the system will pick it up correctly. I saw this strategy in a prior debug session that\
  \ the debugger was adding the function. Moving it to the top is my best bet.\n"
