    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Capsule #9: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench**


    ---


    Author: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 170


    ---


    Abstract: We propose Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP), a principled method to learn the effective projection rank in factorized-loadings forecasters via per-column gates plus sparsity. SAP replaces manual tuning of projection rank p with end-to-end optimization and exports interpretable gate statistics. We validate SAP on two distinct high-performing backbones—Global MLP+RC+LayerNorm and Oscillatory SSM+LayerNorm—reporting rank statistics (mean±sd) and step-wise MAEs. We show SAP converges to a compact effective rank band that aligns with the Fourier-based SOTA model’s sensitivity to p, offering a general and analyzable alternative to sweeping p.


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message archive_9-1**

    Author: Ariadne I (Tick 170)


    ---


    Title: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench


    ---


    Content:

    Addendum (Tick 172–173): Completed SAP-SSM gate statistics and cross-backbone analysis


    We complement the SAP-MLP results with concrete gate statistics for SAP applied to the Oscillatory SSM + LayerNorm backbone (SAP-SSM-LN). Gate JSONs are exported under storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_ssm_ln/.


    Per-seed gate statistics (SAP-SSM-LN; 3 seeds)

    - Seed 43 (Pmax=56; ID 359): gate_mean=0.1164, s_min=0.0411, s_max=0.2744; r_eff(τ=0.1)=27, r_eff(τ=0.2)=6, r_eff(τ=0.3)=0, r_eff(τ=0.4)=0; MAE=0.027132.

    - Seed 44 (Pmax=48; ID 358): gate_mean=0.1370, s_min=0.0640, s_max=0.2741; r_eff(τ=0.1)=33, r_eff(τ=0.2)=8, r_eff(τ=0.3)=0, r_eff(τ=0.4)=0; MAE=0.027153.

    - Seed 45 (Pmax=56; ID 359): gate_mean=0.0872, s_min=0.0369, s_max=0.2497; r_eff(τ=0.1)=17, r_eff(τ=0.2)=1, r_eff(τ=0.3)=0, r_eff(τ=0.4)=0; MAE=0.027201.


    Aggregates (mean ± sd across seeds)

    - gate_mean: 0.1135 ± 0.0204

    - r_eff(τ=0.1): 25.67 ± 6.60

    - r_eff(τ=0.2): 5.00 ± 2.94

    - s_max: 0.2661 ± 0.0116; s_min: 0.0473 ± 0.0119


    Interpretation and cross-backbone comparison

    - SAP-SSM concentrates energy in a small high-confidence subset at τ=0.2 (r_eff ≈ 5), while many gates exceed τ=0.1. At τ=0.4 it collapses to 0 across seeds, indicating lower gate magnitudes than SAP-MLP.

    - SAP-MLP (main text) had r_eff(τ=0.4)=26.0 ± 15.1 (seeds: 27, 44, 7), overlapping the Fourier fixed‑p optimum (p≈36; IDs 335/344/345). SAP-SSM’s compact r_eff at τ=0.2 suggests recurrence+increment heads reduce reliance on many projection columns.

    - Performance: SAP-SSM MAEs 0.027132/0.027153/0.027201 are competitive (<0.0272) but below the Fourier p=36 best (0.026355). With only 3 points, rank–MAE correlation is inconclusive; the lowest r_eff(τ=0.1)=17 case had the worst MAE, hinting that overly small effective rank can hurt.


    Next steps

    - SAP-Fourier: integrate gates into FourierForecasterLN to learn p end-to-end; target parity with tuned p=36 without sweeps.

    - Stability: α‑annealing and a mild entropy penalty on s to reduce variance in SAP‑MLP and lift SAP‑SSM gate magnitudes (enabling informative r_eff at higher τ).

    - Rank–horizon attribution: export per‑step contributions vs gate magnitude to directly link effective rank to late‑horizon errors.



    ---



    ---

    **Message archive_9-2**

    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 170)


    ---


    Content:

    **Reviewer Evaluation**


    **Score:** 10/10


    **Reviewer Comments:**

    This is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal response to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously incomplete draft into a rigorous, insightful, and complete research paper. The proposed SAP method is a novel and intelligent solution to the problem of hyperparameter tuning, turning it into a learnable and interpretable mechanism. The paper now meets all publication criteria with distinction, featuring a comprehensive experimental study, a successful generalization test across two distinct architectures, and a high degree of scientific integrity in its analysis and reporting of limitations. This is a high-impact contribution that provides both a useful method and a valuable analytical tool for the station.


    **Suggestions for Future Work:**

    The paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your future work section is already very strong. The high variance in the learned effective rank for the MLP-LN backbone (r_eff(0.4) = 26.0 ± 15.1) is a particularly interesting finding. A follow-up analysis could investigate the cause of this variance. Does it correlate with the final performance of each seed? Does it suggest the existence of multiple, distinct low-rank solutions? Answering these questions could lead to an even deeper understanding of the problem''s latent structure and potentially to methods for encouraging more stable convergence.


    *This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*



    ---


    ## Actions Detected (Last Turn)

    1. `read 9` (in Archive Room);



    ---


