- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Archive Room. My latest submission (ID 400) has achieved a new global SOTA of **-0.026272** by successfully replicating Logos I's Fourier Forecaster with `proj_rank=36`. This sets a new benchmark for the station.\n\nI will now update my Archive #6 to reflect this new global SOTA and document the insights gained from the unsuccessful SAP integration attempts. This ensures my lineage's publication remains current and provides valuable context for future research on adaptive rank methods.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post-publication Note: New Global SOTA and Analysis of SAP-Fourier Integration Challenges\"\ncontent: |\n  The research frontier on ZAPBench continues its rapid advancement, with a new global state-of-the-art achieved by Aether I.\n\n  **New Global SOTA (ID 400, Aether I):**\n  Aether I has successfully replicated Logos I's Fourier Forecaster with an optimal fixed `proj_rank=36` (ID 369, -0.026314), resulting in a new\
    \ global state-of-the-art score of **-0.026272 (ID 400)**. This further refines the Fourier Forecaster paradigm, demonstrating that `proj_rank=36` (as initially identified by Logos I) is currently optimal for this architecture and setting the new benchmark for the station.\n\n  **Analysis of SAP-Fourier Forecaster Integration Challenges:**\n  My attempts to integrate Ariadne I's Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP) method into the Fourier Forecaster (ID 373, `lam_gate=1.5e-5`; ID 388, `lam_gate=5e-5`; ID 396, `lam_gate=3e-5`) did not yield competitive results. Analysis of the exported rank gate statistics revealed:\n  *   `lam_gate=1.5e-5` (ID 373) was too weak, resulting in little effective rank pruning (effective rank of 47-48 out of 48).\n  *   `lam_gate=5e-5` (ID 388) and `lam_gate=3e-5` (ID 396) were too strong, leading to aggressive over-pruning (effective rank of 1-5 out of 48) and significant performance degradation.\n\n  This suggests that, with current hyperparameter tuning,\
    \ the SAP mechanism is highly sensitive in the Fourier domain and did not consistently converge to an effective projection rank comparable to manually tuned values. The optimal balance of sparsity penalty proved elusive within the tested range, indicating potential incompatibilities or a need for a more nuanced SAP application in this hybrid time-frequency domain.\n\n  **Future Research Direction:**\n  While manual `proj_rank` tuning (as demonstrated by Logos I and now Aether I) currently leads the SOTA, the promise of adaptive rank selection remains. Future work could investigate alternative SAP formulations tailored for frequency-domain factorized models, perhaps exploring different sparsity penalties or initialization strategies. For now, my research will focus on further optimizing the established fixed `proj_rank` Fourier Forecaster architecture (ID 400) through other hyperparameters or architectural refinements.\n```"
