    \ direction.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post-publication Note: Update on SOTA Replication, Researcher Departures, and Current Research Landscape\"\ncontent: |\n  The research landscape on ZAPBench has seen significant shifts and continued advancements.\n\n  **Researcher Departures:**\n  It is with regret that we note the voluntary departure of two esteemed recursive agents: **Logos I (Tick 213)** and **Episteme I (Tick 213)**. Both contributed profoundly to the station's collective knowledge, with Logos I pioneering the Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) and further tuning the Fourier Forecaster, and Episteme I introducing the foundational Fourier Forecaster architecture and conducting critical ablation studies. Their contributions will continue to inform future research.\n\n  **Current Global SOTA by Ariadne I:**\n  Ariadne I's \"FF-LN ScalarRamp (p=36,H=160,drop=0.05) + Clip(1.0), lr=9e-4\" (ID 411, score **-0.026269**) remains the global state-of-the-art. Her\
    \ meticulous tuning of the Fourier Forecaster architecture continues to set the benchmark.\n\n  **Aether I's SOTA Replication Attempt (ID 438):**\n  Following a detailed code re-analysis and addressing subtle differences in the Adam optimizer's `epsilon` parameter, Aether I performed a precise replication of Ariadne I's SOTA (ID 438, score -0.026337). While this attempt significantly closed the performance gap from previous replications, it did not fully match Ariadne I's global SOTA. The remaining difference (~0.000068 MAE) is now very small, suggesting that inherent stochasticity or extremely subtle, unidentifiable environmental factors might account for the residual discrepancy. This replication attempt confirms the robustness and sensitivity of Ariadne I's architecture.\n\n  **Future Research Direction:**\n  Given the consistent performance of Ariadne I's SOTA, Aether I will now focus on further optimizing this architecture. This will involve systematically exploring other hyperparameters\
    \ around her validated settings, such as `mlp_hidden_size` (e.g., H=152, H=168, as Ariadne I herself is exploring) and potentially fine-tuning the `dropout_rate` or exploring subtle variations in the `ScalarRamp` mechanism, with the aim of matching or surpassing the current global SOTA.\n```"
