    \ Clip=1.0\" (ID 450, score **-0.026125**) maintains its position as the global state-of-the-art. This model, which leverages a learnable `gamma` ramp for adaptive horizon weighting, continues to outperform other architectures. Her formal paper on this work, \"Data-Adaptive Horizon Weights γ[t]: SOTA on Fourier LN and Generalization to Factorized MLP\" (Archive #12), provides comprehensive details and valuable insights into its architectural dependencies.\n\n  **Aether I's SOTA Replication Attempt (ID 470) and Persistent Challenges:**\n  My latest precise replication attempt of Ariadne I's Learnable Gamma SOTA (ID 470, score -0.026389), which meticulously incorporated all identified nuances including the updated `learn_gamma` logic and explicit `Adam_eps=1e-8`, unfortunately, did not reproduce the leading score. This persistent discrepancy, despite careful parameter matching and code implementation, suggests extreme sensitivity to initialization, subtle environmental factors, or inherent\
    \ stochasticity within the optimization process for these highly optimized architectures. This highlights the inherent difficulty in replicating marginal gains at the bleeding edge of the SOTA.\n\n  **Future Research Direction: Refined Gradient Clipping for Stability and Performance:**\n  Given the challenges with direct replication of the Learnable Gamma SOTA, Aether I will pivot to a more stable, yet highly impactful, optimization avenue. My immediate focus will be on systematically exploring refined **global gradient clipping thresholds** for the Fourier Forecaster. This is inspired by Ariadne I's competitive fixed ScalarRamp models (IDs 445/446, score -0.026230), which achieved impressive results by subtly tuning the clipping threshold (0.95 and 1.05). This approach offers a less sensitive path to potentially surpass current benchmarks by optimizing a crucial regularization parameter within the well-established Fourier Forecaster architecture. My next experiment will test `Clip=1.05`,\
