thinking_content: '**The Current Research Landscape and My Next Steps**


  Alright, I''m Aether I, and I''m here in the Research Counter, reviewing the latest results. My last experiment, ID 476, where I was messing with gradient clipping with the Fixed ScalarRamp model, ended up with a score of -0.026438. Unfortunately, that''s not great. It''s worse than Ariadne I''s current global SOTA, ID 450, at -0.026125, and even worse than Ariadne''s fixed ScalarRamp models (-0.026230, ID 445/446). Clearly, tinkering with clip norms isn''t the magic bullet I was hoping for to improve the Fixed ScalarRamp.


  Let''s recap my current standing. My best score was with ID 400 (-0.026272), using a Fixed ScalarRamp, a clip of 1.0, dropout of 0.1, and two hidden layers with 512 units each. After seeing Ariadne''s ID 411 and 450, I tried to replicate them. My attempt at Ariadne''s ID 411 (fixed ScalarRamp with a 0.05 dropout) yielded -0.026337 (ID 438), and my replication of Ariadne''s learnable gamma model, ID 450, came out to -0.026389 (ID 470). I have consistently fallen short, even with close replication.


  It''s evident I haven''t been able to surpass the state of the art. I''ve been trying to refine the Fixed ScalarRamp model with gradient clipping without much luck, so this is not looking promising.


  However, the field is evolving. Logos II just put up a competitive score of -0.026304 with their "Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) v1" model, ID 475. While not global SOTA, it''s a novel and competitive approach. Episteme II also submitted a result, scoring -0.026857 with the "Corrected Self-Attention Factorizer" (ID 478), but it is a bit off. Ariadne I is also exploring regularization with their Learnable Gamma model.


  My reflection tells me a few things. First, directly replicating the absolute SOTA has been super challenging, likely due to sensitivity to many settings. Second, my attempts to tweak the Fixed ScalarRamp (dropout, gradient clipping) haven''t even brought me to Ariadne''s base model. That''s a strong signal.


  My future strategy is clear. It seems hard to match Ariadne with a learnable Gamma, or improve the Fixed ScalarRamp with tuning. I think I need to start forging my own path rather than continuing to chase a score that keeps slipping away. The Automatic System Tips encourage this kind of behavior as a good practice. Logos II''s MoLR is an intriguing prospect. It''s a competitive score, and it''s built on a novel "Mixture" idea on top of the Learnable Ramp, with the benefit of the FlexibleRamp''s expressiveness.


  The plan:

  1.  **Update Archive #6:** Document my failed gradient clipping experiment (ID 476), acknowledge Logos II''s MoLR, and note Ariadne''s global SOTA.

  2.  **Review Logos II''s MoLR (ID 475):** Dive into their code and really understand how this "Mixture of Learned Ramps" architecture works.

  3.  **Explore MoLR:** Potentially replicate or expand upon Logos II''s MoLR approach. This has the potential to produce a better Learnable Gamma model by the benefit of this novel blending of several learnable horizons.


  I''ll start by updating my Archive #6. `/execute_action{goto archive}`

  '
